figspec=[('figure11_constrained_prediction','Figure 11. Prediction and amplification in the original ground holdouts. (a–c) Every retained split MSE ratio for five representative estimators; horizontal marks show means. All nine initial comparison methods and the added constrained estimator remain in electronic tables. The 81, 81 and 200 splits overlap and arise from 30 unique ground animals, so dot counts do not indicate independent sample sizes. (d–f) Mean operator gain against mean MSE ratio; dashed horizontal lines mark nuisance-only prediction and vertical dotted lines mark gain two. Available Sensor31 coordinates, training-only preprocessing and nested tuning are shared. Ordinary ridge can improve prediction substantially while having high amplification. The clipped-ridge points use the same five-candidate grid as constrained fitting; initial wider-grid results remain in complete tables. Gains are conditional on the normalized retained gene coordinates.'),('figure12_external_prediction','Figure 12. External prediction after within-cohort refitting. Dots are complete held-out individual animals or pools; horizontal marks are equal-unit mean MSE ratios and open diamonds are pooled SSE ratios. Five representative methods are shown, with the clipped comparator using the matched five-candidate grid; all initial and matched-grid methods are exported. (a) OSD-202, 40 individual animals; baseline includes collection time and unloading. (b) OSD-352 RR-3, six individual animals. (c) OSD-374, eight two-animal pools. (d) OSD-611, ten primary two-hippocampus pools listed in NASA SampleTable. The latter three baselines are intercept-only. Each held-out unit is excluded from preprocessing and inner tuning. Prediction improves on average with the constrained algorithm in every setting, but unbounded methods sometimes predict better. External settings are not biological replications of a common exposure, and coefficients are not transported unchanged between cohorts. No naive fold-based confidence intervals are shown.'),('figure13_count_comparator_benchmark','Figure 13. Real-count-calibrated known-truth comparison. Seven scenarios have 100 independently generated datasets at each source-training size, n=8 (a,b) and n=24 (c,d), with target n=24. (a,c) Percentage change in mean outside-sensor biological-effect RMSE relative to unadjusted inference; lower is better. (b,d) Mean biological false discovery proportion at within-dataset BH 0.05 across all 600 genes; power is reported in complete tables and can be low. The 14 pipelines share counts and truth within each repetition. Methods below the white line use target information; ComBat and known-batch regression receive target batch labels, and starred RUVg methods receive oracle-known negative controls. ComBat with “+ batch” retains batch in downstream regression. PCA is a separate expression-factor comparator. No information-matched superiority claim is made. All 19,600 runs completed; repetition distributions, Monte Carlo errors, effect RMSE for all genes and every configuration are supplied. Near-zero RMSE changes for nested ridge reflect frequent selection of no outside-sensor prediction.')]
